WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.804
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:13.570
DOMINIC PREUSS:
Thank you, everyone.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:15.150
My name is Dominic Preuss.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:19.220
I look after our storage
database and capacity products

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:20.340
on Google Cloud platform.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.620
I'm very excited today,
day three of the conference

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.540
which has been an
incredible conference,

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:28.860
to talk about how Google
has reinvented databases

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.220
and how those people coming
to Google Cloud Platform

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.350
can learn from us and take
advantage of the things

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:35.141
that we've learned.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:45.160
So let's start talking about
the early days of Google.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.080
People often say that
Google was late to cloud.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:53.290
And while Google got into the
public cloud space in 2014,

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:56.170
Google has been running one
of the largest, private cloud

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:59.600
infrastructures for
almost 15 years.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.170
And so even though
we've only been

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:05.050
working with our external GCP
customers for about four years,

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:08.920
we have 15 years of
learnings and technologies

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:12.280
we develop over time that
really have made it possible

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.440
for us to work with
these GCP customers

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.210
and enable things that they
haven't been able to do before.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.990
So the purpose of this
talk is to take you

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:23.440
through Google's journey, see if
that maps to some of the things

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.810
that you're seeing as customers
in some of the journeys

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:27.200
you're going through.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.300
And then we'll talk to one of
the customers that has gone

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.475
through this journey on GCP.

00:01:32.475 --> 00:01:33.850
And then we'll
take a little time

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:35.850
to do a demo because all
good talks have a demo.

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:43.070
For those 15 years,
Google was internally

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:46.490
building technologies
that are use cases.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.100
And we were hitting
use cases that

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.350
were a little bit different
than the rest of the world

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:51.290
was seeing.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:53.360
So we would develop
these technologies,

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.280
and we wanted to push
these technologies out

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:56.465
to the outside world.

00:01:56.465 --> 00:01:58.760
But prior to Google
Cloud Platform,

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.610
the only way for us to do
this was via white papers.

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:04.080
And so we published
lots of white papers.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.770
So if you go back to 2002, we
published the original Google

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:08.600
File System white paper.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.750
So this was what we had learned
running a distributed storage

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.360
system that we could push
back to the rest of the world.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:15.620
We filed that with Bigtable.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:17.870
And we're going to talk a
little bit about the origins

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:19.310
of Bigtable in this talk.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:21.295
And then that spawned
the NoSQL movement

00:02:21.295 --> 00:02:22.670
and a lot of the
things that have

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:26.330
happened in the H-based space
and in the Hadoop space.

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:28.940
Fast forward a little bit,
we published the Spanner

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:31.490
in F1 papers in 2012.

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:36.120
And then finally, we got into
the Google Cloud Platform time.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.200
So now we were actually able
to take these technologies

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.600
that we had developed and
that we'd offered back

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.220
to the community
as white papers,

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.400
and offer them as actual
cloud services, public cloud

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.420
services.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:50.240
So BigQuery is the
same technology that

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:51.770
was in the Dremel white paper.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:53.390
Cloud Bigtable is
the same technology

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:55.342
that was in the Cloud
Bigtable white paper.

00:02:55.342 --> 00:02:57.800
And then Cloud Spanner is the
combination of the technology

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.770
that we talked about in
the Spanner and F1 papers.

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:02.450
And so really we've
been able to take

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:05.880
these 15 years of experience
and then leverage those

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.760
as we've gotten into
the public cloud

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.960
to help customers build
new and exciting things.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.850
So with that, I'm very
excited to bring up

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:16.590
Chris Taylor to the stage.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:17.940
Chris is an early Googler.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.632
He was one of the original
authors of Spanner

00:03:20.632 --> 00:03:22.590
and as our tech lead for
all structured storage

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.510
products, not only
Google Cloud Platform,

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:25.680
but at Google in general.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.296
Welcome, Chris.

00:03:27.296 --> 00:03:29.268
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:35.590
So now I get to embarrass Chris.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.540
So I really want to
start at the beginning.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.081
Because one of the
things that people

00:03:39.081 --> 00:03:41.179
often ask us about is why?

00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:43.220
Why did Google have to
build it's own technology?

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:45.709
So first thing for
Chris, so why did Google

00:03:45.709 --> 00:03:47.500
build its own database
technologies instead

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.390
of using what was available?

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:51.880
CHRIS TAYLOR: Well, so I think
the thing Google has always

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:55.000
had to deal with in
its history is scale.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.310
A lot of our
applications have been

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:59.170
internet facing and
internet search,

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:03.500
and they just sort of scale
with the size of the internet.

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:07.060
And when you look at
the Bigtable paper,

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:10.240
it's full of URLs and
signals and there was really

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:12.850
nothing we could pull off
the shelf for internet scale.

00:04:21.062 --> 00:04:23.270
DOMINIC PREUSS: And one of
the things that we learned

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.200
is that as we were
doing Bigtable

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.260
and as we were going
through the use cases,

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:29.468
I think one of the things
we saw was that it ended up

00:04:29.468 --> 00:04:31.310
being a really good
tool for building

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:32.810
other things at Google.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:36.569
CHRIS TAYLOR: Yeah, you
started to see us branching out

00:04:36.569 --> 00:04:38.360
a little bit into more
things, many of them

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:39.560
were facing the internet.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:43.830
They were Google customers and
Google has a lot of customers.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:48.440
And so you find that
Bigtable is a good hammer

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:52.280
to use on all sorts of
problems that you come across.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.170
And a good example of
this is sort of early

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:56.080
in my personal history.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:59.000
When I joined Google, I worked
on a team called Google Alerts.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.880
And Google Alerts
sends you email

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:03.320
when the web search
results change

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:05.340
for a query of your choice.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:07.715
My first project Google was
learning to run Bigtable.

00:05:07.715 --> 00:05:09.590
And the reason I was
learning to run Bigtable

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:12.030
was because we had a
MySQL back end that

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.030
was getting a little
uncomfortable at the number

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:16.970
of users, the number of
registered queries that we had.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:18.925
And so I did a port.

00:05:18.925 --> 00:05:19.800
DOMINIC PREUSS: Cool.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.830
And one of the things
you like to say to me

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:23.570
is that in the
early days, we were

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:25.250
responsible for
indexing the internet

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:27.002
and that doesn't
really fit in MySQL.

00:05:27.002 --> 00:05:29.210
And so you know, that's a
common problem that we had.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:30.640
CHRIS TAYLOR: That's correct.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:31.370
DOMINIC PREUSS:
And for the back,

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:33.000
we're having a little trouble
with the monitor screen.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.520
So if you guys could bring
up the monitor screen,

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:35.436
that would be awesome.

00:05:35.436 --> 00:05:37.160
Thank you.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:39.480
So OK, that's kind of the
early days of Bigtable.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:45.360
So kind of stepping forward, it
sounds like in the early days,

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.150
we were basically shipping
binaries in containers

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:51.350
just to teams to run
their own Bigtable.

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.859
And obviously today,
we run very differently

00:05:53.859 --> 00:05:55.150
in a much more centralized way.

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:58.170
So kind of how did Google
make that decision?

00:05:58.170 --> 00:05:59.990
Why did we move to
centralized services?

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:01.130
So how does that work?

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:01.963
CHRIS TAYLOR: Right.

00:06:01.963 --> 00:06:03.572
So again, going back
to Google Alerts.

00:06:03.572 --> 00:06:05.030
When I first started
with Bigtable,

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:07.190
the Bigtable team was
handing me a container

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:09.380
and I was meant to go run it.

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:10.970
I was on a two person team.

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:15.885
And I was on call for a Bigtable
cell which was entertaining.

00:06:15.885 --> 00:06:17.510
I was sending mail
to the Bigtable team

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:19.274
every couple of weeks
because I needed

00:06:19.274 --> 00:06:20.690
learn about the
command line flags

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:23.150
and I needed to figure
out how to do upgrades.

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:26.700
And I was behind on the
upgrades, let's be honest.

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:29.224
DOMINIC PREUSS: You're never
behind on anything, Chris.

00:06:29.224 --> 00:06:30.890
CHRIS TAYLOR: So there
were a few things

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:33.320
that Bigtable saw in
moving to services here.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:35.150
And there is to
that earlier point,

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:40.109
saving a lot of unnecessary
specialization and toil.

00:06:40.109 --> 00:06:41.900
And there's a certain
agility that Bigtable

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.750
gets from controlling
its own release cycles,

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:46.880
being able to do upgrades
for its customers,

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:50.390
we get to change more
things than we would expect.

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:54.522
This move to services was
a painful thing for us.

00:06:54.522 --> 00:06:56.230
The first versions
didn't work that well.

00:06:56.230 --> 00:06:58.630
There was a loss of
control for the teams.

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:02.390
The service didn't necessarily
get immediately better.

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:05.430
But this is something that we
iterated a lot on over time.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:08.600
And over a couple of
years, because Bigtable

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:10.050
could see the
customer experience,

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:11.390
we could change things quickly.

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:13.440
Bigtable got a ton better.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.210
100x improvements in throughput,
huge progress in tail latency,

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:19.980
cost reductions,
adding features.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:23.750
When I came back to Bigtable
in 2009, creating a table

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:25.700
was a trivial thing.

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:28.720
You could just show
up, click, get table.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.590
Really not that far
off from the experience

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:35.030
you get today in public
managed databases.

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:39.200
So overall, the move to services
was a huge success for Google.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:41.120
It's very, very rare
for anyone at Google

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.830
to be running
their own database.

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:46.035
And really this experience,
making Bigtable into a service,

00:07:46.035 --> 00:07:47.660
was such a success,
that when we looked

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:50.470
at an offering like Spanner that
we built a little bit later,

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:52.600
we designed that from
scratch to run as a service.

00:07:54.772 --> 00:07:56.230
DOMINIC PREUSS: As
we went through,

00:07:56.230 --> 00:07:58.396
we wanted to show a little
bit of historical photos.

00:07:58.396 --> 00:08:01.740
So the prior photo was actually
when the internal Spanner

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:03.240
service went to GA.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.470
You saw Chris was the
tall guy in the back.

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:08.437
And then this was actually
one of the first outages.

00:08:08.437 --> 00:08:10.270
So the team is working
with a customer who's

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:13.300
the smiling guy in the
blue shirt, who is actually

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:15.320
debugging the problems
with the team.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.620
So the pros and cons of
centralized services.

00:08:19.999 --> 00:08:21.790
So we've talked a little
bit about Bigtable

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:23.081
and some of the things learned.

00:08:23.081 --> 00:08:24.550
So why build Spanner?

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:26.290
And I think the
question that I always

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:29.230
get is, why did we think
Spanner was possible, right?

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:30.880
CAP Theorem, all
these other things.

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:34.630
So why did we think that this
is something we could do?

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:36.460
CHRIS TAYLOR: So
I'd like to say it's

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:38.740
that we're visionaries
of some kind,

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:41.055
but it's not really very true.

00:08:41.055 --> 00:08:43.179
Instead, we were kind of
dragged to the realization

00:08:43.179 --> 00:08:46.210
that NoSQL and
eventual consistency

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:49.240
weren't working for all
of the Bigtable customers.

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:51.160
Google changed a lot
over this period.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:54.150
We were getting into
things like Gmail, Google+,

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:56.560
personalized search, and
consistency was starting

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:59.100
to really matter to
some of those things.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:02.050
At the same time, Google's
SQL Native Ads and Payments

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:06.640
businesses were growing in
size, growing in sophistication.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:08.650
They were using MySQL,
and it was frankly

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:09.629
struggling to keep up.

00:09:09.629 --> 00:09:11.920
We were sharding it really
heavily, needing to re shard

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:14.147
it from time to time.

00:09:14.147 --> 00:09:15.730
And then also we
were seeing this sort

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:18.040
of steady drumbeat
of serious bugs, even

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:20.360
the occasional major outage.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:23.020
Which were basically caused by
eventual consistency ambushing

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:25.550
developers.

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:28.660
And so sitting in
the Bigtable team,

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:31.090
we were seeing applications
building consistency,

00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:34.390
transactions, indexes,
and even SQL engines

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:36.970
as layers on top of Bigtable.

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:39.250
And we came to the realization
that the hard problems

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:41.410
we were avoiding
weren't gone, they

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:44.160
were just pushed to customers.

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:47.750
And so while Spanner in its end
state looks big and ambitious,

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:50.140
it didn't sort of
spring fully-formed out

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:51.250
of someone's head.

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:53.950
It was the next thing we
could do based on what

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:56.740
we were seeing built around us.

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:59.530
I would also say that Spanner
was an inflection point for us

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:02.840
in technical
infrastructure at Google.

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:06.400
It was when we decided our job
was to take on as much storage

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:08.870
complexity as we could handle.

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:12.580
And this was really so that
Google's many, many businesses

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:14.980
could move faster and do more.

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:19.996
The more we took on, the more
they could not have to do.

00:10:19.996 --> 00:10:22.120
And we're really not done
with this process either.

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.160
One of the exciting things
about coming to work every day

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:25.870
is that I can see the
impact that Spanner

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:28.420
has on Google's
business and I know

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:31.595
there's just more we can do
and more that we're working on.

00:10:31.595 --> 00:10:32.470
DOMINIC PREUSS: Cool.

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:33.700
This is actually one
of my favorite photos,

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:35.830
so why don't you give the
background in this photo?

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:36.621
CHRIS TAYLOR: Sure.

00:10:36.621 --> 00:10:38.050
So this was early
days at Spanner.

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:41.740
We had a big nightly test
that ran on 40 or 50 machines.

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:44.680
We called it systest.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:46.960
Sort of randomized,
really stressful thing.

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:49.900
We ran one copy of it
at night at that time.

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:52.090
And this was our
board of post-it notes

00:10:52.090 --> 00:10:53.320
to show how we were doing.

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:56.710
So this little arrow points
to the week we were on.

00:10:56.710 --> 00:10:59.500
So that would would
have been a Wednesday.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:02.267
It was kind of a
rolling list thing.

00:11:02.267 --> 00:11:03.850
So every day I would
put up a post-it.

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:05.132
It was an x or a check.

00:11:05.132 --> 00:11:07.090
If it was an x, you can
see little marks in pen

00:11:07.090 --> 00:11:10.560
for what went wrong that day.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:12.275
DOMINIC PREUSS: A
lot of x's on there.

00:11:12.275 --> 00:11:13.900
CHRIS TAYLOR:
Fortunately, we've exited

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:16.350
this phase of our history.

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:19.120
And we now run significantly
more testing every night

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:22.360
and it comes up a lot greener.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:24.982
But it was fun.

00:11:24.982 --> 00:11:25.815
DOMINIC PREUSS: See?

00:11:25.815 --> 00:11:28.570
Spanner starts the same
way everything else does.

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:30.670
So as we talked
about, we've been

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:31.920
doing this for about 15 years.

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:34.130
We've talked about
Bigtable and Spanner.

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:36.450
And so what are the
things that we've

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:39.330
learned kind of running
this centralized service

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:41.310
over the last 15 years
that we think is--

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:43.366
that you found surprising
that you kind of keep

00:11:43.366 --> 00:11:45.990
in the back of your pocket when
you think about these problems?

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:47.850
CHRIS TAYLOR: Sure.

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:50.070
So I think the main
thing that I take away

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:53.490
is an appreciation for just how
hard it is to develop and scale

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:55.204
a managed database service.

00:11:55.204 --> 00:11:57.120
You know, I don't know
if you can see my hair.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:58.620
I cut it short
when I was getting

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:00.040
ready for this presentation.

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:01.230
There was a lot
more gray in it now

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:03.813
than there is in the picture you
can see me of me on the board

00:12:03.813 --> 00:12:04.410
here.

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:06.100
DOMINIC PREUSS: We've documented
your hair turning gray

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:07.099
through these processes.

00:12:07.099 --> 00:12:08.181
CHRIS TAYLOR: Yes.

00:12:08.181 --> 00:12:12.120
So it's been over a decade
since the Bigtable paper,

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:13.980
a decade of developing
Spanner but half

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:15.660
a decade since the paper.

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:17.370
And when you get
into the code, you

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:19.859
can see that while the
20,000 foot architecture is

00:12:19.859 --> 00:12:21.400
the same as what we
documented there,

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:22.774
almost all the
code is different.

00:12:22.774 --> 00:12:24.400
There's a lot more of it.

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:26.520
And we've learned
a lot about why

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:31.170
simpler alternatives to what
we do are just catastrophic.

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:33.900
And this is really an
under-rated benefit

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:37.590
of using a high scale service
like Spanner or Bigtable.

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:40.140
We've found that the only
way you can scale a Spanner

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:43.650
or Bigtable 10x in
customers, bytes, or QPS,

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:47.930
is to decrease outages per
customer, byte or QPS by 10x.

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:50.820
And anything else and the
number of failures you have just

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:54.210
grows and crushes you as
the service maintainer.

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:58.020
And that's the treadmill we're
on here in GCP databases.

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:02.190
But if you're a customer, you
likely are not scaling by 10x.

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:05.490
You probably aren't even
that big to begin with.

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:08.760
But you really feel the 10x
availability and reliability

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:11.930
that had to go into the
service during that time.

00:13:11.930 --> 00:13:15.810
And that's just kind of
math working in your favor.

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:17.350
Generalizing from
it a little bit,

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:19.370
and you know I mentioned
this a little earlier,

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:22.200
Google's gained a lot of
flexibility and agility

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:26.280
by pushing complexity into its
first party database services

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:28.560
and taking it out
of applications.

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:31.080
That leaves the service
very challenging to build,

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:34.350
but if you can do the
work and get it right,

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:38.970
that amortizes really nicely
across all of your users.

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:40.949
DOMINIC PREUSS: It's
been really interesting.

00:13:40.949 --> 00:13:42.490
Obviously you've
seen this evolution,

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:45.570
but Google with 500
engineers or 1000 engineers

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:47.700
is very different
than the 50,000.

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:50.680
And so running services
for 50,000 engineers

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:55.180
is very different scale problem.

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:55.680
Great.

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:58.670
And so now you and I have
been working together

00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:01.250
on this journey into
the public cloud,

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:03.620
plenty of bumps along
the way, disagreements,

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:05.300
and collaboration.

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:08.914
But other things
that we've learned,

00:14:08.914 --> 00:14:11.330
what are these learnings you
think apply to what we're now

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:12.620
seeing with our GCP customers?

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:16.822
And the journeys that we're
seeing from our GCP customers?

00:14:16.822 --> 00:14:18.530
CHRIS TAYLOR: What
we're I think learning

00:14:18.530 --> 00:14:22.220
is that there's not much to this
story that is Google specific.

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:24.530
Not every customer is building
a search engine, that's

00:14:24.530 --> 00:14:26.570
only a couple of companies.

00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:30.380
But every application has a
database or storage somewhere.

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:33.170
And the fundamentals of what
you want out of a database

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:34.730
really don't change.

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:36.760
You'd rather someone
else operate it.

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:37.880
You want it fast.

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:39.440
You want it reliable.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:41.930
And you don't want to spend
your app's complexity budget

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:44.610
on limitations of your
database platform.

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:46.550
We also haven't talked
much about security,

00:14:46.550 --> 00:14:49.380
but the list of requirements
for that gets longer every year

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:51.200
and that's on every
customer all the time.

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:52.616
DOMINIC PREUSS:
Apparently there's

00:14:52.616 --> 00:14:55.100
this new thing called GDPR
that people are talking about.

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:56.390
That's new.

00:14:56.390 --> 00:14:58.250
CHRIS TAYLOR: So we
put years of work

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:01.070
into being good at
that stuff, right?

00:15:01.070 --> 00:15:04.340
These problems are
really near and dear.

00:15:04.340 --> 00:15:05.617
And we work on them every day.

00:15:05.617 --> 00:15:07.700
And we're excited to bring
some of those solutions

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:10.920
to Google Cloud.

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:13.480
Our best work, and really
this hearkens back to the why

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:16.489
did we build Spanner
stuff, is when

00:15:16.489 --> 00:15:18.530
we can study what our
customers are trying to do,

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:20.510
what they're building.

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:24.140
And see what seems like
the right thing to build.

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:26.760
That's when we excel.

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:28.730
Cloud Platform's
a new era for us.

00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:31.556
We've been doing the private
cloud thing for a long time.

00:15:31.556 --> 00:15:33.680
But we're excited to work
with all of our customers

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:36.920
on a system we think is really
the future of public cloud

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:37.640
databases.

00:15:40.391 --> 00:15:41.390
DOMINIC PREUSS: Awesome.

00:15:41.390 --> 00:15:44.060
So Chris, thank you so much
for the historical context.

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:45.780
Let's give him a
round of applause.

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:46.518
Thank you.

00:15:46.518 --> 00:15:48.012
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:52.571
Great, so that's about
what Google has learned.

00:15:52.571 --> 00:15:54.320
And I think what's
most helpful for people

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:57.440
is here is the journey that
other customers have gone down.

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:00.350
So I'm very excited bringing
Daniel Spoonhower, a.k.a.

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:03.845
Spoons, CTO of LightStep,
to talk about their journey

00:16:03.845 --> 00:16:04.470
into the cloud.

00:16:06.339 --> 00:16:07.380
DANIEL SPOONHOWER: Great.

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:10.185
I'm excited to be here and
talk about a little bit of what

00:16:10.185 --> 00:16:12.525
LightStep does, how
we built it, and how

00:16:12.525 --> 00:16:14.900
we use some of the tools that
Dominic and Chris were just

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:17.150
talking about.

00:16:17.150 --> 00:16:19.629
Right, that's me.

00:16:19.629 --> 00:16:21.170
Almost everybody
does call me spoons.

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:23.750
I feel like that's the first
question everyone asks.

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:24.830
I used to be a Googler.

00:16:24.830 --> 00:16:27.380
Now I'm a LightStepper.

00:16:27.380 --> 00:16:29.589
And I guess I like to
build developer tools.

00:16:29.589 --> 00:16:31.880
That's kind of what I've been
doing over and over again

00:16:31.880 --> 00:16:35.160
in a bunch of different places.

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:38.900
So I wanted to start
with a Google paper

00:16:38.900 --> 00:16:42.130
also, not one of the papers
that these guys mentioned.

00:16:42.130 --> 00:16:43.880
This is a paper that
one of my co-founders

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:45.724
wrote almost 10 years ago now.

00:16:45.724 --> 00:16:48.140
Was called Dapper and it talked
about Google's distributed

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:50.660
tracing infrastructure.

00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:53.150
So Dapper was a
monitoring tool that

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:55.820
was built at Google to help
Google engineers understand

00:16:55.820 --> 00:17:00.230
how requests would flow through
the system that had been built.

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:01.940
Each request would
hit potentially

00:17:01.940 --> 00:17:04.550
thousands of machines,
thousands of services,

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:06.589
and all of that data was
then being aggregated

00:17:06.589 --> 00:17:08.992
and flowed back to the user.

00:17:08.992 --> 00:17:10.450
And understanding
where it was slow

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:12.170
could have been in
any of those services.

00:17:12.170 --> 00:17:13.970
And this tool was
and continues to be

00:17:13.970 --> 00:17:15.950
really critical in
building applications that

00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:20.028
are composed from these
many different services.

00:17:20.028 --> 00:17:21.319
Fast forward a couple of years.

00:17:21.319 --> 00:17:23.027
Now it's not just
Google that is building

00:17:23.027 --> 00:17:24.319
these kinds of systems.

00:17:24.319 --> 00:17:26.480
And a lot of other
folks have started

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:30.590
breaking their applications
from monolithic entities

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:32.194
into smaller pieces.

00:17:32.194 --> 00:17:33.610
Either microservices,
server-less,

00:17:33.610 --> 00:17:36.060
all kinds of things like that.

00:17:36.060 --> 00:17:38.360
But the point is that
once we've broken them up,

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:40.790
it's easier to build the
pieces independently.

00:17:40.790 --> 00:17:43.340
It's easier to deploy
those pieces independently.

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:45.720
And it's easier to scale
them independently.

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:48.175
And that sounds great, right?

00:17:48.175 --> 00:17:49.550
Maybe if you've
done this, you're

00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:51.300
going to disagree that
everything's great.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:54.350
And maybe if you can guess from
the picture, that maybe not

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:56.780
everything's great.

00:17:56.780 --> 00:17:58.394
Does anyone know
Honest Status Page?

00:17:58.394 --> 00:17:59.560
It's not a real status page.

00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:01.700
It's like the status
page that you wish

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:03.560
your service providers had.

00:18:03.560 --> 00:18:05.370
Anyway, this is one
of my favorite tweets.

00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:07.490
"We replaced our monolith
with microservices

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:11.180
so that every outage can
be like a murder mystery."

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:14.300
And the thing is, breaking
up the application

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:16.485
was great in terms of
being able to build it.

00:18:16.485 --> 00:18:18.860
But be able to understand it,
especially understanding it

00:18:18.860 --> 00:18:22.070
from an end user's perspective,
just got a lot harder.

00:18:22.070 --> 00:18:25.160
What we've done is take a story
which had one sort of actor

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:27.222
in a sense, and
we split that role

00:18:27.222 --> 00:18:28.430
into a whole bunch of pieces.

00:18:28.430 --> 00:18:29.920
And now if you were to read
any one of those rules,

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:31.045
it wouldn't make any sense.

00:18:31.045 --> 00:18:35.100
You really need to see the big
picture and the whole story.

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:38.240
So how did Dapper work?

00:18:38.240 --> 00:18:43.790
Well, roughly for every
request, for every search,

00:18:43.790 --> 00:18:47.660
for every RPC between services,
Dapper captured timing data,

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:49.220
it captured logs,
and the connections

00:18:49.220 --> 00:18:50.510
between those services.

00:18:50.510 --> 00:18:52.370
This is a lot of data.

00:18:52.370 --> 00:18:55.370
And if you're Google handling
a bajillion requests a second,

00:18:55.370 --> 00:18:57.574
it's a lot a lot of data.

00:18:57.574 --> 00:18:59.240
And so Dapper's
approach to this problem

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:02.040
was actually pretty
simple and effective.

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:05.220
It was just throw out
99.9% of the data.

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:08.780
Or maybe even 99.99% of the
data immediately without even

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:09.920
looking at it.

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:13.640
That might sound a little
bit crazy, but even

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:16.640
with that tiny sliver of data,
there's still a lot you can do.

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:19.310
So for example,
Google runs analyzes

00:19:19.310 --> 00:19:21.830
of the samples it's taken to
understand and answer questions

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:24.170
like, on average, what was
causing web search to be

00:19:24.170 --> 00:19:25.430
slow over the last week?

00:19:25.430 --> 00:19:27.110
Was it query rewriting?

00:19:27.110 --> 00:19:28.820
Was it image search results?

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:32.000
Was it a different
type of search result?

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:34.286
Or maybe it was just
good old network latency.

00:19:34.286 --> 00:19:35.660
These are all
important questions

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:39.200
and they're critical to
prioritizing engineering work

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:42.080
and improving performance.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:44.060
There's a tension,
though, between operating

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:46.457
at this kind of scale
and the features

00:19:46.457 --> 00:19:48.540
that you might be able to
offer in other contexts.

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:51.076
So at LightStep, we took a
pretty different approach.

00:19:51.076 --> 00:19:53.450
And I want to talk about that
from the perspective of one

00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:56.070
of our customers.

00:19:56.070 --> 00:19:59.070
So Lyft has deployed
LightStep and uses

00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:02.270
LightStep to understand
their application.

00:20:02.270 --> 00:20:06.140
They've built using a
microservice architecture.

00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:10.040
And they use this really
cool service mesh technology

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:13.010
called Envoy to
basically proxy requests

00:20:13.010 --> 00:20:14.230
between those microservices.

00:20:14.230 --> 00:20:15.140
And one of the
things that you can

00:20:15.140 --> 00:20:16.556
do with that that's
pretty awesome

00:20:16.556 --> 00:20:19.250
is that meant that we could
integrate really quickly

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:22.460
between Lyft and LightStep, and
get kind of instant visibility

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:25.460
into how requests were
passing from the app all

00:20:25.460 --> 00:20:29.130
the way through all of their
different microservice systems.

00:20:29.130 --> 00:20:31.574
So how does LightStep
differ from Dapper?

00:20:31.574 --> 00:20:32.990
The short answer
is that LightStep

00:20:32.990 --> 00:20:34.530
looks at all of the data.

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:37.760
So for Lyft, that means
we analyzed more than 100

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:40.280
billion RPCs every day.

00:20:40.280 --> 00:20:42.320
That's something
like tens of millions

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:45.170
of microservice
requests every second.

00:20:45.170 --> 00:20:47.499
And we think if you're
a Lyft engineer,

00:20:47.499 --> 00:20:49.040
your firefighting
an outage, you want

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:51.422
to understand what's
happening, every single one

00:20:51.422 --> 00:20:52.880
of those requests
could potentially

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:53.762
be important to you.

00:20:53.762 --> 00:20:55.220
And so we want to
make sure that we

00:20:55.220 --> 00:20:57.260
have all of that data
there available to you

00:20:57.260 --> 00:21:00.130
to answer those questions.

00:21:00.130 --> 00:21:03.500
To do this, we had to take
a pretty different approach

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:04.985
to what was done in Dapper.

00:21:04.985 --> 00:21:06.860
And we had to put a lot
of thought and effort

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:09.470
into building a really
efficient system for processing

00:21:09.470 --> 00:21:11.830
all of this data.

00:21:11.830 --> 00:21:13.490
This is part of our team.

00:21:13.490 --> 00:21:15.860
We've taken what we've learned
from Dapper, what we've

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:18.590
learned from using and building
other kinds of monitoring

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:19.310
tools.

00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:21.530
And we're trying to take
all of that experience

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:24.050
and bring it to our mission
to help developers understand

00:21:24.050 --> 00:21:25.820
application performance
as they adopt

00:21:25.820 --> 00:21:27.812
these modern architectures
and platforms.

00:21:27.812 --> 00:21:29.270
I would love to
talk about the work

00:21:29.270 --> 00:21:30.686
that our team is
doing in building

00:21:30.686 --> 00:21:33.110
these high volume, real time
event processing systems,

00:21:33.110 --> 00:21:35.319
but you'll have to come
talk to me later about that.

00:21:35.319 --> 00:21:37.860
I want to take a minute and talk
about the parts of LightStep

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:40.820
that are not super high volume,
real time event processing

00:21:40.820 --> 00:21:42.290
systems.

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:43.726
We still need to
deploy services.

00:21:43.726 --> 00:21:45.350
We still need to
store our timing data,

00:21:45.350 --> 00:21:48.590
trace data, lot of other
stuff persistently.

00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:51.200
A whole bunch of stuff that
was critical, but not unique

00:21:51.200 --> 00:21:53.660
to our business
and to our product.

00:21:53.660 --> 00:21:55.160
And as LightStep
got off the ground,

00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:56.060
we had to make a lot of choices.

00:21:56.060 --> 00:21:56.660
Where do we build?

00:21:56.660 --> 00:21:57.326
Where do we buy?

00:21:57.326 --> 00:21:59.510
Where do we use open source?

00:21:59.510 --> 00:22:02.840
Where do we reinvent the
wheel or hopefully not?

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:06.230
So I want to tell
you a little story.

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:08.035
We're a monitoring service.

00:22:08.035 --> 00:22:09.410
Maybe you're
wondering what tools

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:11.450
we use to monitor our
monitoring service.

00:22:11.450 --> 00:22:13.607
And of course, there's
a bunch of them.

00:22:13.607 --> 00:22:14.690
LightStep is one of those.

00:22:14.690 --> 00:22:17.000
So we operate a bunch
of different instances

00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:18.110
of LightStep.

00:22:18.110 --> 00:22:20.150
One of those we call
Meta and Meta's job

00:22:20.150 --> 00:22:24.470
is just to keep watch on
the rest of LightStep.

00:22:24.470 --> 00:22:27.590
And at the time of this story,
Meta, like the other instances

00:22:27.590 --> 00:22:30.080
included a Cassandra
deployment that

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:31.670
was used to store
some low frequency

00:22:31.670 --> 00:22:34.730
information to low
frequency time series data.

00:22:34.730 --> 00:22:38.480
And we kept running into
problems with resource use,

00:22:38.480 --> 00:22:40.555
some of the instances
were booming.

00:22:40.555 --> 00:22:41.930
We had some problems
with the way

00:22:41.930 --> 00:22:43.970
that we had deployed it
on top of Kubernetes.

00:22:43.970 --> 00:22:47.270
Which maybe you
can imagine where

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:48.830
this is going a little bit.

00:22:48.830 --> 00:22:51.110
But roughly, what
that all led to

00:22:51.110 --> 00:22:54.280
is some persistent and
reoccurring corruption

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:56.430
in the underlying SSTables.

00:22:56.430 --> 00:22:58.280
And this, in fact, led
to one of the worst

00:22:58.280 --> 00:22:59.624
outages we've ever experienced.

00:22:59.624 --> 00:23:01.790
Luckily, it wasn't in a
customer facing environment.

00:23:01.790 --> 00:23:05.420
But it still felt pretty bad to
have one of our own instances

00:23:05.420 --> 00:23:07.064
down for quite a while.

00:23:07.064 --> 00:23:08.480
And a lot of
engineering time went

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:09.530
into mitigating the problem.

00:23:09.530 --> 00:23:11.655
I think days of engineer's
time just to get us back

00:23:11.655 --> 00:23:12.950
where it was working again.

00:23:12.950 --> 00:23:15.650
And we were not out of
the woods by any means

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:17.210
because a lot of
these configurations

00:23:17.210 --> 00:23:19.060
were also being used in
our other environments

00:23:19.060 --> 00:23:20.893
and they just hadn't
hit these problems yet.

00:23:20.893 --> 00:23:22.460
So we quickly did
some evaluation

00:23:22.460 --> 00:23:24.800
to look at some other options.

00:23:24.800 --> 00:23:26.360
And we ended up with Spanner.

00:23:26.360 --> 00:23:29.120
Maybe not surprising in the
context of where we are today.

00:23:29.120 --> 00:23:31.190
But it actually I
think took us less work

00:23:31.190 --> 00:23:34.560
to migrate from where we were
onto Spanner than it would have

00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:37.380
taken us to build the
expertise and just fix

00:23:37.380 --> 00:23:41.761
the deployments that we had in
production live at the time.

00:23:41.761 --> 00:23:43.510
I think it's really
important to look back

00:23:43.510 --> 00:23:45.176
at the choices we've
made and understand

00:23:45.176 --> 00:23:48.560
what we could have done
better and what we learned.

00:23:48.560 --> 00:23:51.940
In this case, I think it
really reinforced me for me

00:23:51.940 --> 00:23:56.170
that you can't overestimate
the engineering hours that

00:23:56.170 --> 00:23:58.010
will go into understanding
and maintaining

00:23:58.010 --> 00:23:59.218
a distributed storage system.

00:24:01.972 --> 00:24:03.180
Again, this is all hindsight.

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:04.170
I feel really obvious.

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:07.110
But where we were, we were
just making decisions really

00:24:07.110 --> 00:24:08.880
fast as a small company.

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:12.380
But I think this is one of the
instances that I look back on.

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:15.744
And whenever we have a choice
to use a hosted service,

00:24:15.744 --> 00:24:17.410
this is the kind of
thing I think about.

00:24:17.410 --> 00:24:19.050
Because with something
like Spanner,

00:24:19.050 --> 00:24:22.260
I don't think about the
human operational costs

00:24:22.260 --> 00:24:25.770
and I'm confident that we're in
control, that we can scale it

00:24:25.770 --> 00:24:27.660
up as we need, and
we can make choices

00:24:27.660 --> 00:24:32.430
about what other technology we
want to use to complement that.

00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:35.970
So if you kind of look under
the hood at LightStep today,

00:24:35.970 --> 00:24:37.710
this is sort of
that in a nutshell.

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:39.750
Some secret sauce on the top.

00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:45.450
And then Cloud Kubernetes
Cloud SQL Storage and Spanner.

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:47.890
Those are kind of the important
parts of what we have.

00:24:47.890 --> 00:24:49.890
As a monitoring
tool, our customers

00:24:49.890 --> 00:24:53.820
have pretty high expectations
for LightStep's reliability.

00:24:53.820 --> 00:24:56.400
They depend on us to be there
even when their own services

00:24:56.400 --> 00:24:57.816
are not working
so they can better

00:24:57.816 --> 00:24:59.590
understand what's happening.

00:24:59.590 --> 00:25:02.220
So as a service, it's
part of our brand

00:25:02.220 --> 00:25:05.610
to be a reliable,
go-to kind of place.

00:25:05.610 --> 00:25:08.580
We depend on other services
to help fulfill that promise.

00:25:08.580 --> 00:25:11.580
And so I think to take
Spanner as an example,

00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:14.130
I feel really good about
being in multiple regions now

00:25:14.130 --> 00:25:18.120
and knowing that even fairly
large failures in terms

00:25:18.120 --> 00:25:21.325
of a snow storm or
something like that

00:25:21.325 --> 00:25:23.450
are not going to affect
the service that we provide

00:25:23.450 --> 00:25:26.350
to our customers.

00:25:26.350 --> 00:25:29.460
So to kind of
close up, if you're

00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:31.830
adopting any of this
cool technology,

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:35.555
be it Kubernetes,
microservices, Cloud Functions,

00:25:35.555 --> 00:25:36.180
whatever it is.

00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:38.250
But you're having a
little bit of a hard time

00:25:38.250 --> 00:25:40.380
understanding what's happening.

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:42.150
First, you can go talk
to some folks that

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:44.441
are right down at the end of
the hall, the Cloud Native

00:25:44.441 --> 00:25:45.900
Computing Foundation.

00:25:45.900 --> 00:25:47.820
They are incubating
Open Tracing.

00:25:47.820 --> 00:25:49.890
Open Tracing is
an open source API

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:51.930
for getting data out
of an application

00:25:51.930 --> 00:25:54.902
into a platform like LightStep.

00:25:54.902 --> 00:25:56.610
They're also incubating
Envoy and a bunch

00:25:56.610 --> 00:25:58.362
of other really cool stuff.

00:25:58.362 --> 00:25:59.820
And then secondly,
of course I'd be

00:25:59.820 --> 00:26:01.611
more than happy to talk
to folks about what

00:26:01.611 --> 00:26:02.850
we're doing at LightStep.

00:26:02.850 --> 00:26:05.100
I'm really excited about the
technology we're building

00:26:05.100 --> 00:26:08.272
and the way that we can really
help push forward the way

00:26:08.272 --> 00:26:10.480
that people are building
these kinds of applications.

00:26:10.480 --> 00:26:13.474
So with that, I wanted to
thank everyone for listening.

00:26:13.474 --> 00:26:15.390
I want to thank Dominic
and everyone at Google

00:26:15.390 --> 00:26:16.681
for having me up here on stage.

00:26:16.681 --> 00:26:17.860
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:17.860 --> 00:26:19.276
DOMINIC PREUSS:
Thank you so much.

00:26:22.270 --> 00:26:24.060
Great.

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:26.890
In these sessions, I always
find what's the most helpful

00:26:26.890 --> 00:26:30.400
for GCP customers is to hear
the experience and learnings

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:32.350
from other GCP customers.

00:26:32.350 --> 00:26:34.946
And so I really appreciate
Spoons being here to do that.

00:26:34.946 --> 00:26:36.820
Now we've talked a little
bit about the past,

00:26:36.820 --> 00:26:38.095
we've talked about the
present, so not we're

00:26:38.095 --> 00:26:39.928
going to talk a little
bit about the future.

00:26:39.928 --> 00:26:42.601
So I'm very excited to bring up
Deepti and Steve from the Cloud

00:26:42.601 --> 00:26:44.350
Spanner team and they're
going to take you

00:26:44.350 --> 00:26:46.058
through a preview of
some new things that

00:26:46.058 --> 00:26:48.454
are coming in Cloud Spanner.

00:26:48.454 --> 00:26:51.720
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA:
Thanks, Dominic.

00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:52.700
Hi, everyone.

00:26:52.700 --> 00:26:54.040
My name is Deepti Srivastava.

00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:57.970
I am the product manager
for Cloud Spanner.

00:26:57.970 --> 00:27:01.630
Just to give a quick history,
I was also responsible

00:27:01.630 --> 00:27:04.990
for launching
Spanner internally.

00:27:04.990 --> 00:27:08.361
That photo was from my
phone that you saw earlier.

00:27:08.361 --> 00:27:10.360
So my new Noogler project
was launching Spanner.

00:27:10.360 --> 00:27:12.800
So I have some history in it.

00:27:12.800 --> 00:27:14.840
And I'm joined by
Steve Middlekauff

00:27:14.840 --> 00:27:18.080
who is a senior engineer
in the Spanner team.

00:27:18.080 --> 00:27:21.400
And he's going to show us
a few cool things that we

00:27:21.400 --> 00:27:22.210
have coming out.

00:27:25.610 --> 00:27:27.500
So I talked about this
in my talk yesterday,

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:30.740
but basically as we heard
from Chris and Dominic,

00:27:30.740 --> 00:27:32.720
we've been building
Spanner for a long time.

00:27:32.720 --> 00:27:34.910
And of course, the
history of databases

00:27:34.910 --> 00:27:36.160
is 30 plus years long.

00:27:36.160 --> 00:27:39.560
So we could have
added features like--

00:27:39.560 --> 00:27:42.830
there was a ton of features that
we could have added and exposed

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:44.480
to our cloud customers,
but we really

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:49.140
wanted to be deliberate about
what we offer on the Cloud

00:27:49.140 --> 00:27:51.140
Platform to our
customers based on what

00:27:51.140 --> 00:27:53.100
they're looking for, right?

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:55.080
So we added multi-region,
et cetera last year.

00:27:55.080 --> 00:28:00.720
And then there's two really
highly anticipated features

00:28:00.720 --> 00:28:04.370
that I get asked about a lot
that we're here to show today.

00:28:04.370 --> 00:28:07.400
This is not a promise
of timelines or sort

00:28:07.400 --> 00:28:09.170
of announcements of
the feature launch,

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:10.640
it's more about what
we've been working on

00:28:10.640 --> 00:28:12.639
and what we hope to have
in the next few months.

00:28:15.075 --> 00:28:17.450
Before we get into it, we also
launched the import export

00:28:17.450 --> 00:28:19.910
functionality for Cloud Spanner
which was the top feature

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:21.320
demand last week.

00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:23.210
And we demoted it
also yesterday.

00:28:23.210 --> 00:28:26.030
So but today, I want to
talk about the things we're

00:28:26.030 --> 00:28:28.260
working on that we
think are super cool.

00:28:28.260 --> 00:28:33.350
So what Steve has here
is our UI console.

00:28:33.350 --> 00:28:34.850
If you aren't
familiar with Spanner,

00:28:34.850 --> 00:28:38.720
this is the UI that we have
and we start with the instance

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:41.140
page on our UI.

00:28:41.140 --> 00:28:45.800
Cloud Spanner instances are like
in other situations clusters.

00:28:45.800 --> 00:28:49.610
So basically, this is our
unit of replication topology

00:28:49.610 --> 00:28:50.980
and instance in isolations.

00:28:50.980 --> 00:28:53.160
So you can have multiple
databases inside a Cloud

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:55.470
Spanner instance.

00:28:55.470 --> 00:28:58.130
So in this case, we're looking
at a DML demo database.

00:28:58.130 --> 00:29:00.546
So yeah, the first functionality
we're going to talk about

00:29:00.546 --> 00:29:04.400
is DML, which is doing inserts,
updates and deletes in SQL.

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:08.750
So just to be clear, you have
the ability to mutate data,

00:29:08.750 --> 00:29:10.890
to insert, update,
delete through the API,

00:29:10.890 --> 00:29:14.330
but we didn't have this
DML capability in SQL

00:29:14.330 --> 00:29:16.590
and now we are working
on releasing it soon.

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:18.914
So we're excited
to talk about that.

00:29:18.914 --> 00:29:20.330
So in this case,
our demo database

00:29:20.330 --> 00:29:21.600
is a ticket info database.

00:29:21.600 --> 00:29:23.720
And as can see, the
schema on the side,

00:29:23.720 --> 00:29:27.590
it has a count event, ticket,
and all those kinds of tables.

00:29:27.590 --> 00:29:29.930
Fairly standard
relational database.

00:29:29.930 --> 00:29:34.580
And we also have two indexes on
the event table, event by date

00:29:34.580 --> 00:29:37.390
and event by multi-event ID.

00:29:37.390 --> 00:29:40.004
So now let's actually
query the database.

00:29:40.004 --> 00:29:41.670
Now, this is something
you can do today.

00:29:41.670 --> 00:29:43.670
So you can go to
the query console

00:29:43.670 --> 00:29:45.390
and you can actually
type in queries.

00:29:45.390 --> 00:29:48.780
And you can get the
results right there.

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:51.530
So Steve typed in a
query to get information

00:29:51.530 --> 00:29:54.060
for all of the events
in the event table.

00:29:54.060 --> 00:29:56.960
And as you can see, we
have no multi-event ID

00:29:56.960 --> 00:29:58.256
set in this table.

00:29:58.256 --> 00:30:00.380
So let's look at the explain
plan, again, something

00:30:00.380 --> 00:30:01.490
we have today.

00:30:01.490 --> 00:30:03.530
Where you can look
at the SQL explain,

00:30:03.530 --> 00:30:05.340
as you can see it's
using the index

00:30:05.340 --> 00:30:09.800
to be efficient about
fetching this data.

00:30:09.800 --> 00:30:13.490
So now, what if we wanted
to change the multi-event ID

00:30:13.490 --> 00:30:17.240
to say, Cloud Next
for all events

00:30:17.240 --> 00:30:19.160
that happened as
of Tuesday, which

00:30:19.160 --> 00:30:21.410
is when Cloud Next started.

00:30:21.410 --> 00:30:26.210
So we have an update
query, update where.

00:30:26.210 --> 00:30:29.610
And voila, we have a DML
query that completed.

00:30:29.610 --> 00:30:31.500
So let's look at
the explain plan.

00:30:31.500 --> 00:30:33.884
And as you can see, in
our explain plan now

00:30:33.884 --> 00:30:35.300
we have applied
mutations so we're

00:30:35.300 --> 00:30:38.070
supporting explain in
the DML case as well.

00:30:38.070 --> 00:30:40.100
So let's look at
what we actually

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:42.470
did so we're not
faking this out,

00:30:42.470 --> 00:30:45.400
like the data
should have changed.

00:30:45.400 --> 00:30:47.200
It's a live demo.

00:30:47.200 --> 00:30:52.190
And it should say multi-event
ID for all the events

00:30:52.190 --> 00:30:56.380
that are as of Tuesday.

00:30:56.380 --> 00:30:57.970
And as you can see,
I wasn't lying.

00:30:57.970 --> 00:31:01.120
We can actually make
DML statements work.

00:31:01.120 --> 00:31:02.950
Now the cool thing
about this is that you

00:31:02.950 --> 00:31:05.320
can do this for
petabyte scale databases

00:31:05.320 --> 00:31:06.760
or very, very small databases.

00:31:06.760 --> 00:31:08.380
And you can do it right
in the query console.

00:31:08.380 --> 00:31:09.796
But of course,
when we release it,

00:31:09.796 --> 00:31:13.240
we're going to have it
in our API in the UI

00:31:13.240 --> 00:31:16.020
and also through
our JDBC drivers.

00:31:16.020 --> 00:31:20.094
So just again, to be clear,
we do have a JDBC driver

00:31:20.094 --> 00:31:21.760
right now, which is
read-only because we

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:23.170
didn't have DML statements.

00:31:23.170 --> 00:31:25.870
And when we launch, we will
have the JDBC driver as well.

00:31:25.870 --> 00:31:29.180
And we plan to have ORM
support as we go along

00:31:29.180 --> 00:31:31.780
Spring Boot, hibernate,
et cetera, once we

00:31:31.780 --> 00:31:33.790
have this functionality.

00:31:33.790 --> 00:31:37.510
So hopefully I answered
number two of the most

00:31:37.510 --> 00:31:40.880
frequently asked
feature requests.

00:31:40.880 --> 00:31:44.740
Now let's look at
another feature

00:31:44.740 --> 00:31:47.740
that I'm super excited
about that we're also

00:31:47.740 --> 00:31:49.750
planning to launch soon.

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:54.100
This is called the Query
Statistics feature.

00:31:54.100 --> 00:31:56.150
So we're back at
our instance page.

00:31:56.150 --> 00:31:58.600
And as you can see, it's
the same demo instance

00:31:58.600 --> 00:32:02.410
that has the same ticket
information as before,

00:32:02.410 --> 00:32:03.840
the same schema.

00:32:03.840 --> 00:32:06.670
Now, as we enter the
instance in this case,

00:32:06.670 --> 00:32:09.700
now we have a new
tab, Query Stats.

00:32:09.700 --> 00:32:11.660
And if you click into
the Query Stats tab,

00:32:11.660 --> 00:32:15.220
you can see that we are
basically showing you

00:32:15.220 --> 00:32:17.380
all the queries that
took up the most

00:32:17.380 --> 00:32:20.680
resources in the last,
in this case, minute.

00:32:20.680 --> 00:32:22.180
So we have multiple
buckets in which

00:32:22.180 --> 00:32:25.090
we aggregate this information,
one minute, 10 minute, one

00:32:25.090 --> 00:32:26.260
hour, 24 hours.

00:32:26.260 --> 00:32:27.890
So hopefully it
gives you a good view

00:32:27.890 --> 00:32:31.790
of how things are behaving
over that period of time.

00:32:31.790 --> 00:32:34.630
But we're most interested in
the last minute for right now.

00:32:34.630 --> 00:32:40.000
So this is sorted by the
top 10 queries by CPU.

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:42.310
Let's click into the first one.

00:32:42.310 --> 00:32:44.980
So now we have an
info pane panel there

00:32:44.980 --> 00:32:48.160
that shows us more information
about this particular query

00:32:48.160 --> 00:32:51.290
that's been taking the most
CPU in the last one minute.

00:32:51.290 --> 00:32:54.100
So it's been taking
261 CPU seconds.

00:32:54.100 --> 00:32:56.490
And as you can see
in our info panel,

00:32:56.490 --> 00:32:59.230
there is more information
like average latency,

00:32:59.230 --> 00:33:01.490
rows scanned, rows
sent, et cetera.

00:33:01.490 --> 00:33:03.640
So in this case, you can
see that the rows scanned

00:33:03.640 --> 00:33:05.800
and the rows sent
are the same, which

00:33:05.800 --> 00:33:08.980
means that the query isn't
doing any aggregations,

00:33:08.980 --> 00:33:10.434
or filters, or joins.

00:33:10.434 --> 00:33:12.100
And if you look at
the query text, which

00:33:12.100 --> 00:33:15.460
is right there so
you can actually

00:33:15.460 --> 00:33:17.620
inspect that particular
query as well,

00:33:17.620 --> 00:33:21.750
you can see that it's not
doing any of those things.

00:33:21.750 --> 00:33:24.774
The intuition is accurate here.

00:33:24.774 --> 00:33:26.690
One thing to talk about
here is that the query

00:33:26.690 --> 00:33:29.080
is using a parameterized value.

00:33:29.080 --> 00:33:31.300
So it's using bind parameters.

00:33:31.300 --> 00:33:34.570
And this is actually a useful
thing to do in general, both

00:33:34.570 --> 00:33:36.850
because it helps you with
performance and latency

00:33:36.850 --> 00:33:40.510
because the query engine
can then have an opportunity

00:33:40.510 --> 00:33:42.250
to cache this query
plan so that you

00:33:42.250 --> 00:33:44.650
don't have to rebuild the
query plan every time you

00:33:44.650 --> 00:33:45.710
run the query.

00:33:45.710 --> 00:33:47.410
And if you saw the
statistics earlier,

00:33:47.410 --> 00:33:51.520
the query is actually run about
150,000 times last minute,

00:33:51.520 --> 00:33:55.150
but it didn't have to rebuild
the whole query plan each time

00:33:55.150 --> 00:33:58.480
even though the account ID
value could be different.

00:33:58.480 --> 00:34:00.860
So it will obviously
help it perform better.

00:34:00.860 --> 00:34:02.764
But also, it helps
with collecting

00:34:02.764 --> 00:34:03.930
these kinds of stats, right?

00:34:03.930 --> 00:34:06.520
Because the engine understands
that this is the same query

00:34:06.520 --> 00:34:08.840
that you're running over
and over and over again.

00:34:08.840 --> 00:34:14.050
So now let's look at another
query that's more interesting.

00:34:14.050 --> 00:34:18.010
So this query took
34 CPU seconds

00:34:18.010 --> 00:34:20.719
and so much less than
the previous one.

00:34:20.719 --> 00:34:24.010
And it ran only 1,000 times.

00:34:24.010 --> 00:34:26.679
But in this case, the row
scan and send are different.

00:34:26.679 --> 00:34:29.050
And obviously, that means
it's doing something

00:34:29.050 --> 00:34:30.850
more interesting with
fetching the data

00:34:30.850 --> 00:34:32.800
and then returning
it to the user.

00:34:32.800 --> 00:34:35.024
So look, we have the
query opened right

00:34:35.024 --> 00:34:36.190
there so we can take a look.

00:34:36.190 --> 00:34:41.679
And what it's doing is
it's fetching the seating

00:34:41.679 --> 00:34:43.030
capacity for all the venues.

00:34:43.030 --> 00:34:47.590
And Steve is moving
faster than I can talk.

00:34:47.590 --> 00:34:53.260
But in case you wanted to debug
the query as it was running

00:34:53.260 --> 00:34:54.820
in either development
or production,

00:34:54.820 --> 00:34:57.100
you would want to open
it and run it and see

00:34:57.100 --> 00:34:58.180
what it does, right?

00:34:58.180 --> 00:35:01.300
So you can click on
a button right there

00:35:01.300 --> 00:35:02.560
and in that Query Stats page.

00:35:02.560 --> 00:35:06.970
It will open this
SQL API query API

00:35:06.970 --> 00:35:09.820
and you can actually
inspect the query that you

00:35:09.820 --> 00:35:11.290
can run it as Steve ran.

00:35:11.290 --> 00:35:14.380
And you can see that we have
a Beijing Stadium category

00:35:14.380 --> 00:35:17.300
1 and category 2 both
have 6,000 seats.

00:35:17.300 --> 00:35:19.180
And if we look at the
query explain plan

00:35:19.180 --> 00:35:22.810
because we want to see what is
it doing, why is it doing it?

00:35:22.810 --> 00:35:24.870
You can see that it's
applying all these filters

00:35:24.870 --> 00:35:27.910
and doing a cross apply
hash aggregate join.

00:35:27.910 --> 00:35:32.381
So the idea here is
that as you're both

00:35:32.381 --> 00:35:33.880
developing your
system and as you're

00:35:33.880 --> 00:35:36.280
looking at your system
and its performance,

00:35:36.280 --> 00:35:40.410
you can inspect right there
what the thing is doing.

00:35:40.410 --> 00:35:43.780
And this is a plan for
this particular invocation

00:35:43.780 --> 00:35:45.760
of the query, but it
can give you an idea of,

00:35:45.760 --> 00:35:47.549
let's say, it's
taking a long time

00:35:47.549 --> 00:35:49.840
and you want to see is it
using the right index or not?

00:35:49.840 --> 00:35:51.280
Or do I need to create an index?

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:55.340
All those kinds of things you
can see by quick inspection.

00:35:55.340 --> 00:35:58.610
So these are some of the many
features we're working on.

00:35:58.610 --> 00:36:00.170
If you have feedback
for us, I'll

00:36:00.170 --> 00:36:04.050
